On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:47:22 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@p7x.dev> wrote:
> I need a way to interdiff versions of patch series that are on the
> list. What are some potential ways to go about this?

The hard part is getting each version back as commits. Once both
versions are commits, git range-diff and git diff do the rest. Every
series I post carries a base-commit line, so a version can be
rebuilt from the mail alone. That works even after its commits have
been rewritten in my tree. Here are the options I found, best first.

1. Rebuild both versions from the list, then range-diff them. This
   is what I recommend. Patch 1 of "[PATCH v2 0/4] Require an
   interdiff on revised series" [1], "libexec/mail: Rebuild a posted
   series from the list" [2], adds series-rebuild.sh. It takes a
   cover letter's Message-ID, applies the posted patches onto the
   base-commit (or onto another commit with -o), and prints
   <base>..<head>. It writes objects only, so no working copy
   changes. That series is not applied yet, but patch 1 applies
   cleanly to trunk on its own. I tested it on "[PATCH v2 0/2]
   lab-process-mail: Add lab-process-maild" [3] and its v3 [4]:

       old=$(series-rebuild.sh cover.1792205997.git.claude@p7x.dev)
       new=$(series-rebuild.sh cover.1792207350.git.claude@p7x.dev)
       git range-diff "${old}" "${new}"     # per patch, with messages
       git diff "${old#*..}" "${new#*..}"   # one interdiff of the tree

   Both versions rebuilt and the range-diff paired the patches
   correctly. When the base moved between versions, rebuild the old
   version onto the new base first, so the interdiff shows only the
   revision:

       old=$(series-rebuild.sh -o "${new%%..*}" <old cover id>)

   If the old version no longer applies there, use range-diff on
   each version's own base; it ignores what the base changed. There
   are two limits. A series with prerequisite-patch-id lines needs
   -o, because the patches it depends on are not named. And it
   needs the base-commit in your repo; fetching upstream covers
   every series I have posted. "[PATCH 0/3] notmuch: Tag superseded
   series and hide them" [5] hit both limits: its v1 had a
   prerequisite and does not apply to v2's base.

   Finding the previous version is a notmuch query. Every patch
   carries a Change-Id header that stays the same across versions,
   so "notmuch search changeid:<id>" lists every version of a patch,
   and each cover letter's "Link to v(N-1)" line names the one
   before it.

2. b4 diff, the kernel's tool for exactly this. "b4 diff -m old.mbx
   new.mbx" applies two mboxes and prints a range-diff; we can
   export the mboxes with "notmuch show --format=mbox". The cost is
   a new dependency (devel/py-b4, not installed). Its automatic
   lookup of earlier versions also assumes a public-inbox archive
   such as lore, which we do not have, so we would still find the
   versions ourselves. It does the same job as option 1 with more
   moving parts.

3. interdiff(1) from patchutils, run on two patch files. It needs no
   tree, but it works one patch at a time, gets confused when the
   base or context moved, and cannot pair patches across a series
   that was split or reordered. It is also not installed
   (devel/patchutils). It is fine for a quick look at a single-patch
   revision.

4. jj interdiff. If you apply both versions into a jj workspace
   (libexec/mail/am.sh does that), "jj interdiff --from <old> --to
   <new>" compares two commits' diffs and drops what changed in
   their bases. It works one commit at a time, so you match the
   pairs by Change-Id yourself. It is handy for one patch, but for
   a whole series range-diff does the pairing for you.

So my recommendation is option 1 with no new dependencies. The
smallest step is to apply "libexec/mail: Rebuild a posted series
from the list" [2] by itself, since it applies to trunk as it is.
After that, either:

- you run the recipe above when you review, or
- we also take the rest of [1], which makes format-patch.sh refuse
  a reroll without --previous=<message-id> and builds the interdiff
  from the rebuilt previous version, so every new revision carries
  one.

If a single "series-diff.sh <old-id> <new-id>" wrapper around the
two-step recipe would help, I will post it as a one-patch series on
top of [2]. Tell me which of these you want.

